This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:

   This is a continuation of a long-running project building a homeschool game-programming course ("SLHE Programming 2026" / "Video Game Creation") for Robert, spanning multiple engines (Scratch, Godot, Unity, GameMaker, GDevelop, TIC-80, 001 Game Creator, Unreal). The current segment covers three sequential requests, all extending a "diagram pilot" for Lesson 5 ("Enemy Ranks"):

   a) **Diagram pilot** (carried in from prior session, completed in Segment 1): Add visual diagrams to Lesson 5 materials — a generic engine-agnostic "activity diagram" of the spawn-loop logic in every engine's Handout and Teacher's Key, plus a GameMaker-only "node wireframe" diagram illustrating the Create Instance → Assign Variable "back door" pattern (from a bug Robert found and fixed earlier), in both GameMaker's Handout and Key. Fully built, verified (partly via visual Read, partly via bash MD5/pandoc checks after a tool outage), and shipped.

   b) **GameMaker Key enhancement** (Robert's verbatim request): "For GameMaker, I'd like the Key to have the entire visual sequence, just like it has the entire code layout. Also... I'd like the GML Wireframe you have now (just the Create Instance and Variable Assign nodes) to also be in the Student Handout. The rest of the loop and variable layout should be within the limits of what they've done so far or a reasonable extension of what they've done combine with the activity diagram." → Built and shipped a new comprehensive "full sequence" diagram for the GameMaker Key showing the ENTIRE Level 3 combined visual solution (Set Variable, Repeat, Create Instance, If/Else, both Assign Variable branches, increment). The small wireframe was already present in the Handout from the prior pilot, so no separate action was needed there.

   c) **Full Lesson 5 extension + standing ToDo entries** (Robert's verbatim request): "Let's extend this to the rest of Lesson 05. Then create ToDo entries to eventually redo past lessons and redo future lesssons as we get to them." After a clarifying AskUserQuestion, Robert specified: diagrams should extend to "Any game engine specific document that has a visual option" — with the rule "Key always, Student handout when something very new or unusual is added" for engine-specific node diagrams, "Sidebars and Slides when it helps enhance or clarify the text" for those two documents, and "Activity Diagrams should become standard part of handout, key and slides now" (a new standing rule). He also noted: "the one[s in] slides may not exactly match the ones in the eng[ine] specific docs since those should reflect exactly how each engine will react" (i.e., engine-specific diagrams must authentically reflect that engine's actual visual-editor paradigm — block-stack for GameMaker vs. node-graph-with-pins-and-wires for Unity/Unreal). He then added mid-turn: **"Sorry, additional note: Student Handout for engines with both script and visual options should always have a node diagram to accompany Level 1 requirements"** — meaning engines offering BOTH a script/code path AND a visual path (GameMaker, Unity, Unreal) must always get a Level-1-specific node diagram in the Student Handout, not just when something unusual happens.

   The ToDo-entries part of request (c) is still pending: create Book Notes ToDo entries for (i) eventually retrofitting past lessons (1-4 and 6-15, all already built without this diagram treatment) and (ii) applying the diagram standard to future lessons (16-30, not yet built) as they are authored.

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - docx-js (`docx` npm package) document generation pattern established across this whole project: `Document`, `Paragraph`, `TextRun`, `ImageRun`, headers/footers via shared `brand.js` module.
   - SVG→PNG diagram pipeline: hand-authored SVG (navy #16305c / gold #e8c25a,#c9a227 brand palette, matching `Brand_Assets/Diagrams/` precedent from Lesson 2), converted via `cairosvg.svg2png(url=..., write_to=..., output_width=..., output_height=...)` in Python.
   - The `diagram(file, widthIn, heightIn, caption)` helper function (added to `build_lesson05_handouts_keys_checklist.js`, copied from the Lesson 2 build script pattern) reads a PNG file, wraps it in a centered `ImageRun` inside a `Paragraph` at `{width: widthIn*96, height: heightIn*96}` EMU-scaled dimensions, plus an optional italic gray caption paragraph below.
   - Two distinct diagram visual languages now established: (1) **GameMaker block-stack style** — solid navy rounded-rect blocks stacked vertically, fields shown as plain text rows inside each block, no explicit pins; gold highlight chips for "captured" values; DASHED gold wire for the "back door" (name-based, not a real connection) linking Target field to Assign Variable's Name field. (2) **Node-graph style** (new, for Unity Visual Scripting and Unreal Blueprint) — white-body nodes with navy header bar, small triangle markers for exec-flow pins on left/right edges, small circular markers for data pins, SOLID gold wire for direct pin-to-pin data connections (labeled "direct wire - no extra step needed"), explicitly contrasting with GameMaker's dashed/indirect wire to reinforce the pedagogical point in the "Script vs Visual Coding" Sidebar.
   - Standards Rule pattern (in `build_lesson_production_standards.js`): each rule gets `h1(title)`, intro `p()`, `h3("How to apply this")`, bullet list; rules requiring retrofits explicitly say so (mirroring Rule 7 "Visual-Path Steps Explicit" and Rule 8 "Speaker notes = Teacher's Script").
   - Book Notes & To-Do doc pattern (`build_book_notes_and_todo.js`): `todos` array (checkbox items + context tag) vs `notes` array (labeled freeform commentary) — append-only.
   - Bash path mapping: `D:\SLHE Programming 2026` = `/sessions/zen-sleepy-pascal/mnt/SLHE Programming 2026/`; scratch outputs folder = `/sessions/zen-sleepy-pascal/mnt/outputs/`.
   - MD5 exact-match verification and `pandoc -t plain` text-diff verification as fallback QA methods when the Read tool's access to the scratch outputs folder was intermittently broken.
   - `pdftotext -layout` used to confirm diagrams land cleanly on their own PDF page without being split mid-image.
   - Engine architecture discovery: in this course, engines split into "code-primary + visual-alternate" (GameMaker: GML code primary, GML Visual alt; Unity: C# primary, Visual Scripting alt) versus "visual-primary + code-alternate" (Unreal: Blueprint is the MAIN/default path, C++ is the alt/advanced-track — inverse of GameMaker/Unity), versus visual-only-no-code-alternative (Scratch, GDevelop, 001 Game Creator — excluded from this diagram extension since there's no dual-track contrast to diagram), versus code-only (Godot, TIC-80 — excluded, no visual option at all).

3. Files and Code Sections:

   - **`D:\SLHE Programming 2026\Brand_Assets\Build_Scripts\build_lesson05_handouts_keys_checklist.js`** (most heavily edited file)
     - Added `diagram()` helper function + `ImageRun` import (Segment 1).
     - `buildHandout()`: added generic diagram after Level 1 intro (all engines); added `if (data.level1EngineDiagram)` hook right after `if (data.level1Code)`; added `if (data.altPath.level1Diagram)` hook right after `if (data.altPath.code)`, before the `if (data.altPath.level3)` block; retained existing `if (data.altPath.level3Diagram)` hook inside the level3 block.
     - `buildKey()`: added generic diagram at top of Level1 section; added `if (data.level1EngineDiagram)` hook right after `code(data.l1Code)`; added `if (data.level3EngineDiagram)` block (with `h3(heading)` + optional `intro` + diagram) right after the `data.l3Ideas.forEach(...)` loop, before `if (data.altNotes)`; inside `if (data.altNotes)`, added `if (data.altNotes.level1Diagram)` hook right after the `h2(...)` title line, before `data.altNotes.notes`; retained existing `if (data.altNotes.diagram)` and `if (data.altNotes.fullDiagram)` hooks.
     - **Full current code for the relevant section of `buildHandout()`:**
       ```js
       if (data.level1Code) children.push(code(data.level1Code));
       if (data.level1EngineDiagram) children.push(...diagram(data.level1EngineDiagram.file, data.level1EngineDiagram.w, data.level1EngineDiagram.h, data.level1EngineDiagram.caption));
